### What are the three depth-first traversals?

All three depth-first traversals visit the same tree. The only difference is when the current node is processed relative to the two recursive calls. This makes them variations of one template.

- **Inorder**: recurse left, visit node, recurse right.
- **Preorder**: visit node, recurse left, recurse right.
- **Postorder**: recurse left, recurse right, visit node.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class HierarchyTraversalService {

    static class AccountNode {
        BigDecimal balance;
        AccountNode left, right;
        AccountNode(BigDecimal balance) { this.balance = balance; }
    }

    public static void inorder(AccountNode node, List<BigDecimal> result) {
        if (node == null) return;
        inorder(node.left, result);
        result.add(node.balance);
        inorder(node.right, result);
    }

    public static void preorder(AccountNode node, List<BigDecimal> result) {
        if (node == null) return;
        result.add(node.balance);
        preorder(node.left, result);
        preorder(node.right, result);
    }

    public static void postorder(AccountNode node, List<BigDecimal> result) {
        if (node == null) return;
        postorder(node.left, result);
        postorder(node.right, result);
        result.add(node.balance);
    }
}
```

### When do you use each traversal?

Each traversal order maps to a concrete use case. Knowing which order to use for which task is what interviewers look for.

- **Inorder** – sorted output from a BST. The left-node-right order follows the BST invariant and produces ascending values.
- **Preorder** – cloning or serialising a tree. The root is emitted first, so replaying a preorder sequence rebuilds the same shape.
- **Postorder** – safe deletion or expression evaluation. Children are handled before the parent, so you can free child nodes before the parent or compute operands before the operator.
- **Level-order** – printing by depth, finding shortest paths, connecting level siblings.

```java
// Use case: inorder on BST produces sorted output
//       500
//      /   \
//    200   800
//   /       \
//  100      900
// Inorder: [100, 200, 500, 800, 900] - sorted!
```

### How does the iterative inorder traversal work?

The iterative version uses an explicit stack to replace the call stack. Push all left children until null, then pop and visit, then move to the right child and repeat.

```java
import java.util.Stack;

public static List<BigDecimal> inorderIterative(AccountNode root) {
    List<BigDecimal> result = new ArrayList<>();
    Stack<AccountNode> stack = new Stack<>();
    AccountNode current = root;

    while (current != null || !stack.isEmpty()) {
        while (current != null) {
            stack.push(current);
            current = current.left;
        }
        current = stack.pop();
        result.add(current.balance);
        current = current.right;
    }
    return result;
}
```

### What is Morris Traversal?

Morris Traversal achieves inorder traversal in O(1) extra space by temporarily creating threads (links from a node's inorder predecessor back to it). It modifies the tree structure temporarily and restores it.

```java
public static List<BigDecimal> morrisInorder(AccountNode root) {
    List<BigDecimal> result = new ArrayList<>();
    AccountNode current = root;

    while (current != null) {
        if (current.left == null) {
            result.add(current.balance);
            current = current.right;
        } else {
            // Find inorder predecessor
            AccountNode predecessor = current.left;
            while (predecessor.right != null && predecessor.right != current) {
                predecessor = predecessor.right;
            }
            if (predecessor.right == null) {
                predecessor.right = current; // Create thread
                current = current.left;
            } else {
                predecessor.right = null; // Remove thread
                result.add(current.balance);
                current = current.right;
            }
        }
    }
    return result;
}
// Time: O(n), Space: O(1) - no stack or recursion
```

### How is recursive different from iterative traversal?

| Aspect | Recursive | Iterative (Stack) | Morris |
|---|---|---|---|
| Space | O(h) call stack | O(h) explicit stack | O(1) |
| Simplicity | Very simple | Moderate | Complex |
| Stack overflow risk | Yes, for deep trees | No | No |
| Modifies tree | No | No | Yes, temporarily |
| Production use | Default choice | Deep trees | Memory-constrained |

```java
// Deep tree risk with recursion
// A skewed tree of 100,000 nodes causes StackOverflowError
// Iterative version handles any depth safely
```

### What are the edge cases?

- **Empty tree (null root)** – all traversals return an empty list.
- **Single node** – all three traversals return just that node's value.
- **Left-skewed tree** – inorder and postorder visit nodes bottom-up; recursion depth equals n.
- **Right-skewed tree** – preorder visits nodes in the same order as insertion.

```java
// Edge case: null root
List<BigDecimal> result = new ArrayList<>();
inorder(null, result);
System.out.println(result); // []
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting the null check at the start of the recursive method.

```java
// WRONG: no null check
public static void inorderBuggy(AccountNode node, List<BigDecimal> result) {
    inorderBuggy(node.left, result); // NullPointerException when node is null
    result.add(node.balance);
    inorderBuggy(node.right, result);
}

// CORRECT: null check first
public static void inorder(AccountNode node, List<BigDecimal> result) {
    if (node == null) return;
    inorder(node.left, result);
    result.add(node.balance);
    inorder(node.right, result);
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should be able to implement all three traversals both recursively and iteratively, and explain Morris traversal's threading mechanism. They should know that preorder plus inorder (or postorder plus inorder) uniquely determines a binary tree, which is how serialisation and reconstruction work.

They should also know that the iterative preorder is the simplest (push right then left to the stack), iterative inorder requires the "push all lefts" pattern, and iterative postorder is the trickiest (use a prev pointer or two stacks).
